<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mark the Evangelist</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saint Mark, St Mark</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mark the Evangelist is the traditional author of the Gospel of Mark and, by Christian tradition, founder of the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 828 two Venetian merchants smuggled relics said to be his out of Alexandria to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, where the Basilica of San Marco was built to house them. His winged lion became the emblem of the Venetian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist.jpg" alt="Mark the Evangelist" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scriptorium of the Lorsch Abbey (Hofschule Karls des Großen), Germany · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codexaureus_21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mark the Evangelist is also known as John Mark, and an unbroken tradition, according to William Lane, identifies him with John Mark and with the cousin of Barnabas. Hippolytus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in On the Seventy Apostles, instead distinguishes three figures: Mark the Evangelist, John Mark and Mark the cousin of Barnabas. All three, he says, were among the Seventy Disciples whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> sent out to Judea.</p><p>Coptic tradition holds that Mark was born in Cyrene, in the Pentapolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and that his house hosted the disciples after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Other denominations do not share the belief that he was a Roman African from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to Eusebius of Caesarea, Peter met Mark on his journey through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and took him as companion and interpreter. Mark wrote down Peter's sermons, and so composed the Gospel according to Mark. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> says that after the Council of Jerusalem Mark went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> with Barnabas.</p><p>Tradition holds that Mark then travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and founded the church there, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a>, the Greek Orthodox Church of Alexandria and the Coptic Catholic Church all trace their origins. He is honoured as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Eusebius says that Anianus succeeded him as bishop in the eighth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, probably because Mark's death was near.</p><p>Scholars are divided over the gospel's authorship. It does not name its author, as was common among ancient biographies. The earliest evidence for Mark's authorship comes from Papias, c. 60 – c. 130, whose information modern scholars find hard to interpret. Most scholars favour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as the place of composition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Later Coptic tradition says that Mark was martyred in 68. According to this tradition, the pagans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> resented his efforts to turn the city away from its traditional gods, put a rope around his neck and dragged him through the streets until he died.</p><p>His feast is kept on 25 April by the Catholic and Eastern Orthodox churches, and also by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a> keeps it on Parmouti 30, which falls on the same date in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>. In art Mark is shown writing or holding his gospel, and his symbol is the winged lion. Major shrines include the Basilica di San Marco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Saint Mark's Coptic Orthodox Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mark the Evangelist” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 828 two Venetian merchants smuggled the relics of <b>Saint Mark the Evangelist</b> out of Alexandria, and the saint became the symbol of a city that increasingly acted as an independent state, recognised in the Pactum Lotharii of 840.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Leaders were executed and <b>Saint Mark's</b> banner restored, though Greek rebels led by the Kallergis brothers fought on until 1367.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-saint-titus">Republic of Saint Titus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palestine. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.63,35.15&amp;year=64" aria-label="Palestine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5456 1012 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mark the Evangelist"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5906 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5906 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.63,35.15&amp;year=64">What was here in 64 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
